            <ArticleTitle>3x2 Classroom Goal Structures, Motivational Regulations, Self-Concept, and Affectivity in Secondary School.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>E40</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1017/sjp.2017.37</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The main objective was to analyze relationships and predictive patterns between 3x2 classroom goal structures (CGS), and motivational regulations, dimensions of self-concept, and affectivity in the context of secondary education. A sample of 1,347 secondary school students (56.6% young men, 43.4% young women) from 10 different provinces of Spain agreed to participate (M age = 13.43, SD = 1.05). Hierarchical regression analyses indicated the self-approach CGS was the most adaptive within the spectrum of self-determination, followed by the task-approach CGS. The other-approach CGS had an ambivalent influence on motivation. Task-approach and self-approach CGS predicted academic self-concept (p &lt; .01; p &lt; .001, respectively; R 2 = .134), and both along with other-approach CGS (negatively) predicted family self-concept (p &lt; .05; p &lt; .001; p &lt; .01, respectively; R 2 = .064). Physical self-concept was predicted by the task-approach and other-approach CGS's (p &lt; .05; p &lt; .001, respectively; R 2 = .078). Finally, positive affect was predicted by all three approach-oriented CGS's (p &lt; .001; R 2 = .137), whereas negative affect was predicted by other-approach (positively) and self-approach (negatively) CGS (p &lt; .001; p &lt; .05, respectively; R 2 = .028). These results expand the 3x2 achievement goal framework to include environmental factors, and reiterate that teachers should focus on raising levels of self- and task-based goals for students in their classes.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
